WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.980
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:06.300 --> 00:00:07.800
SAMEER FAROOQUI:
Joep, can you start

00:00:07.800 --> 00:00:12.660
by clarifying what
exactly Google Cloud--

00:00:12.660 --> 00:00:16.743
I'm sorry, what exactly Twitter
is moving to Google Cloud?

00:00:16.743 --> 00:00:18.660
JOEP ROTTINGHUIS: Thanks
for hosting us today.

00:00:18.660 --> 00:00:21.810
We are migrating our ad
hoc and our cold storage

00:00:21.810 --> 00:00:23.500
use cases to cloud.

00:00:23.500 --> 00:00:25.950
That takes about a third of
our Hadoop infrastructure,

00:00:25.950 --> 00:00:28.800
hence our project
name, Partly Cloudy.

00:00:28.800 --> 00:00:30.727
SAMEER FAROOQUI: You
said ad hoc and cold.

00:00:30.727 --> 00:00:31.560
What does that mean?

00:00:31.560 --> 00:00:33.750
Ad hoc is exploratory
data analysis.

00:00:33.750 --> 00:00:34.685
What's cold?

00:00:34.685 --> 00:00:36.810
JOEP ROTTINGHUIS: Yeah, we
have different use cases

00:00:36.810 --> 00:00:38.977
for different-- different
clusters for different use

00:00:38.977 --> 00:00:40.080
cases.

00:00:40.080 --> 00:00:42.640
Original data lands
on RT clusters.

00:00:42.640 --> 00:00:44.550
That's where the
original data comes in.

00:00:44.550 --> 00:00:48.720
Then ad hoc and exploratory use
cases run on ad hoc clusters.

00:00:48.720 --> 00:00:50.640
Production clusters
run the repeat jobs

00:00:50.640 --> 00:00:53.010
that run over, and
over, and over again.

00:00:53.010 --> 00:00:54.960
Those sit on
production clusters.

00:00:54.960 --> 00:00:56.610
And our cold storage
is more for data

00:00:56.610 --> 00:00:59.940
that is less accessed
and less frequently used.

00:00:59.940 --> 00:01:02.190
SAMEER FAROOQUI: Is it fair
to say that the production

00:01:02.190 --> 00:01:05.820
clusters are batch jobs
versus the ad hoc clusters are

00:01:05.820 --> 00:01:07.425
kind of like off the cuff jobs?

00:01:07.425 --> 00:01:10.050
JOEP ROTTINGHUIS: Yeah, most of
the jobs on the Hadoop clusters

00:01:10.050 --> 00:01:12.090
are batch.

00:01:12.090 --> 00:01:14.220
The difference between
production and ad hoc

00:01:14.220 --> 00:01:16.410
is more that the
ad hoc interactive

00:01:16.410 --> 00:01:17.480
and less predictable.

00:01:17.480 --> 00:01:19.625
Whereas production jobs
are the repeat jobs

00:01:19.625 --> 00:01:21.250
that run over, and
over, and over again

00:01:21.250 --> 00:01:22.333
that are more predictable.

00:01:22.333 --> 00:01:23.875
SAMEER FAROOQUI:
Great, and I believe

00:01:23.875 --> 00:01:25.875
you blogged about this
yesterday as well, right?

00:01:25.875 --> 00:01:27.833
JOEP ROTTINGHUIS: Yes,
we have a blog post out.

00:01:27.833 --> 00:01:29.670
And most of those
links will end up being

00:01:29.670 --> 00:01:32.820
on cloud.google.com/twitter.

00:01:32.820 --> 00:01:35.352
SAMEER FAROOQUI: So
why Google Cloud?

00:01:35.352 --> 00:01:36.810
JOEP ROTTINGHUIS:
A couple of years

00:01:36.810 --> 00:01:40.020
ago, we ended up doing a large
evaluation of multiple vendors.

00:01:40.020 --> 00:01:41.730
And we measured many
different things,

00:01:41.730 --> 00:01:43.250
did a lot of benchmarking.

00:01:43.250 --> 00:01:45.620
And particularly the
network capability,

00:01:45.620 --> 00:01:48.210
the ability to be able to
separate storage from compute,

00:01:48.210 --> 00:01:50.880
and to be able to scale
the compute independently

00:01:50.880 --> 00:01:53.592
from the storage, those
were all deciding factors.

00:01:53.592 --> 00:01:55.800
And then one of the other
very large deciding factors

00:01:55.800 --> 00:01:58.320
was the technical capabilities
and the interaction

00:01:58.320 --> 00:02:00.330
with all of the engineers
on the Google side

00:02:00.330 --> 00:02:03.992
that has shown a great
partnership so far.

00:02:03.992 --> 00:02:05.700
SAMEER FAROOQUI: Cool--
so before getting

00:02:05.700 --> 00:02:07.770
into the details of your
Hadoop infrastructure,

00:02:07.770 --> 00:02:11.700
I'd like to get the audience
involved in our discussion.

00:02:11.700 --> 00:02:14.400
Tweet your question
with this hashtag.

00:02:14.400 --> 00:02:17.910
The hashtag will be repeated
throughout the future slides

00:02:17.910 --> 00:02:20.730
on the bottom left.

00:02:20.730 --> 00:02:23.460
Asheem, who's raising
his hand in the front,

00:02:23.460 --> 00:02:26.200
will be sharing your questions
live from Twitter with us

00:02:26.200 --> 00:02:28.700
throughout the panel, but also
at the end of the panel where

00:02:28.700 --> 00:02:32.910
we'll do a dedicated
10 or 15 minutes Q&amp;A.

00:02:32.910 --> 00:02:35.380
So we won't be using
Dory for this session.

00:02:35.380 --> 00:02:39.820
We'll take the questions live
from Twitter with the hashtag.

00:02:39.820 --> 00:02:44.080
So let's talk about Hadoop
as it runs at Twitter

00:02:44.080 --> 00:02:46.700
on prem at scale right now.

00:02:46.700 --> 00:02:49.300
So these are pretty
massive numbers--

00:02:49.300 --> 00:02:53.682
500,000 cores, more than
10,000 node clusters.

00:02:53.682 --> 00:02:55.810
Lohit, can you talk a
bit about the challenges

00:02:55.810 --> 00:02:59.652
involved with running
Hadoop at this scale?

00:02:59.652 --> 00:03:00.610
LOHIT VIJAYARENU: Sure.

00:03:00.610 --> 00:03:03.650
So there are several challenges.

00:03:03.650 --> 00:03:07.930
But I would like to point out
that scalability is probably

00:03:07.930 --> 00:03:10.240
the very top.

00:03:10.240 --> 00:03:14.380
Whatever features we implement
or any new infrastructure

00:03:14.380 --> 00:03:16.990
we plan to build, from
the very beginning we

00:03:16.990 --> 00:03:19.740
consider scalability.

00:03:19.740 --> 00:03:22.930
A couple of years back,
when we started off

00:03:22.930 --> 00:03:26.260
with our Hadoop clusters,
we had about 2,000

00:03:26.260 --> 00:03:27.630
node Hadoop clusters.

00:03:27.630 --> 00:03:32.710
And that, at that time
the largest publicly known

00:03:32.710 --> 00:03:35.530
Hadoop cluster was
about 4,000 nodes.

00:03:35.530 --> 00:03:37.150
And our challenge,
our target, was

00:03:37.150 --> 00:03:39.160
to build a 6,000 node cluster.

00:03:39.160 --> 00:03:46.160
So right from HDFS, to YAM, be
it the applications, libraries,

00:03:46.160 --> 00:03:48.040
for example, shared cache--

00:03:48.040 --> 00:03:50.050
whatever features
we had to build,

00:03:50.050 --> 00:03:51.910
we had to think
about scalability.

00:03:51.910 --> 00:03:54.970
And today we have our
single largest clusters

00:03:54.970 --> 00:03:57.950
are more than 12,500 nodes.

00:03:57.950 --> 00:03:59.242
And we have multiple of them.

00:03:59.242 --> 00:04:01.450
SAMEER FAROOQUI: Yeah, I
mean, that's pretty amazing.

00:04:01.450 --> 00:04:04.660
Is that one single
Hadoop cluster, one name

00:04:04.660 --> 00:04:07.128
node that's serving
out 12,500 nodes?

00:04:07.128 --> 00:04:09.170
LOHIT VIJAYARENU: So that's
a very good question.

00:04:09.170 --> 00:04:10.690
It's actually
multiple name node.

00:04:10.690 --> 00:04:13.870
We could have anywhere between
six to eight name nodes.

00:04:13.870 --> 00:04:16.269
Traditionally, the
architecture of HDFS

00:04:16.269 --> 00:04:20.149
was that a single name
node could not scale.

00:04:20.149 --> 00:04:24.970
So Hadoop community and
we invested a lot of time

00:04:24.970 --> 00:04:26.900
in federation--

00:04:26.900 --> 00:04:29.920
HDFS federation, client
side model tables--

00:04:29.920 --> 00:04:33.175
to make it more scalable
to 12,500 hosts.

00:04:33.175 --> 00:04:34.050
SAMEER FAROOQUI: Wow.

00:04:34.050 --> 00:04:38.410
And Joep, are you running your
Hadoop directly from Apache?

00:04:38.410 --> 00:04:40.322
Or is it a vendor distribution?

00:04:40.322 --> 00:04:42.530
JOEP ROTTINGHUIS: Well, you
can imagine at our scale,

00:04:42.530 --> 00:04:44.322
we're building our own
Hadoop distribution.

00:04:44.322 --> 00:04:46.730
It is very close to Apache.

00:04:46.730 --> 00:04:48.560
So when we first
started with Hadoop 2,

00:04:48.560 --> 00:04:51.620
we were one of the earlier ones,
sort of on 2 oh zero alpha.

00:04:51.620 --> 00:04:53.447
And then we worked
with the community.

00:04:53.447 --> 00:04:54.530
There was a newer release.

00:04:54.530 --> 00:04:56.488
Then we leapfrogged them
and jumped to the 2.2,

00:04:56.488 --> 00:04:57.850
and et cetera, et cetera.

00:04:57.850 --> 00:05:00.920
So we're very close to an
open source distribution,

00:05:00.920 --> 00:05:04.020
maybe a couple of dozen, 200
patches difference, but very,

00:05:04.020 --> 00:05:04.520
very close.

00:05:04.520 --> 00:05:06.020
And those are
typically patches that

00:05:06.020 --> 00:05:07.640
are going upstream, as well.

00:05:07.640 --> 00:05:09.015
SAMEER FAROOQUI:
Yeah, I know you

00:05:09.015 --> 00:05:11.210
guys are a very major
contributor to open source--

00:05:11.210 --> 00:05:11.940
Hadoop as well.

00:05:11.940 --> 00:05:13.650
So thanks for that.

00:05:13.650 --> 00:05:16.070
Vrushali, I'm curious
about the compute cores.

00:05:16.070 --> 00:05:17.830
Half a million compute cores--

00:05:17.830 --> 00:05:21.672
what Apache engines
are you running there?

00:05:21.672 --> 00:05:22.880
VRUSHALI CHANNAPATTAN: Hello?

00:05:22.880 --> 00:05:25.910
So in data platform, we
support a bunch of technologies

00:05:25.910 --> 00:05:27.790
for doing data processing.

00:05:27.790 --> 00:05:30.180
Our production clusters
run Scalding jobs.

00:05:30.180 --> 00:05:33.500
So most of our production
workload is all Scalding.

00:05:33.500 --> 00:05:35.300
In addition to Scalding,
what we've seen

00:05:35.300 --> 00:05:37.030
is people want to use--

00:05:37.030 --> 00:05:39.650
people want to have different
ways of getting at the data.

00:05:39.650 --> 00:05:41.610
They want to use
different technologies.

00:05:41.610 --> 00:05:45.797
So we also have Spark, Hive,
Presto on our ad hoc clusters.

00:05:45.797 --> 00:05:47.630
SAMEER FAROOQUI: So a
variety of them-- what

00:05:47.630 --> 00:05:51.660
about the 300 petabytes of
logical storage, Vrushali.

00:05:51.660 --> 00:05:55.930
What kind of data file
formats are you guys using?

00:05:55.930 --> 00:05:59.590
VRUSHALI CHANNAPATTAN: On HDFS,
we have LZO Thrift and Parquet

00:05:59.590 --> 00:06:01.590
as our main file formats.

00:06:01.590 --> 00:06:04.882
We also have a few
regular text files.

00:06:04.882 --> 00:06:05.840
SAMEER FAROOQUI: I see.

00:06:05.840 --> 00:06:08.510
And, you know, Lohit,
this is something

00:06:08.510 --> 00:06:11.000
that I found really interesting
when I worked with you guys

00:06:11.000 --> 00:06:11.970
on this project--

00:06:11.970 --> 00:06:15.560
that 1.5 trillion messages
a day, and even the 300

00:06:15.560 --> 00:06:16.550
petabytes number.

00:06:16.550 --> 00:06:19.790
How do you get to 1.5
trillion messages a day?

00:06:19.790 --> 00:06:22.790
Surely, there's not quite
that many tweets per day?

00:06:22.790 --> 00:06:26.400
And each tweet is only
about 280 characters.

00:06:26.400 --> 00:06:28.360
How does that build
up to 300 petabytes?

00:06:28.360 --> 00:06:30.690
LOHIT VIJAYARENU: Yeah, so
that's a very good question.

00:06:30.690 --> 00:06:35.040
So the actual tweet text itself
is a very small percentage.

00:06:35.040 --> 00:06:36.920
So whenever users
visit Twitter.com

00:06:36.920 --> 00:06:40.340
or use the Twitter
mobile, there are

00:06:40.340 --> 00:06:44.480
a lot of things they're doing--
the interactions, the timeline,

00:06:44.480 --> 00:06:48.830
the order of tweets they see,
who do follow suggestions,

00:06:48.830 --> 00:06:50.000
the search they do.

00:06:50.000 --> 00:06:53.660
So all these actions generate
something called as events.

00:06:53.660 --> 00:06:57.620
And we collect--
our framework is

00:06:57.620 --> 00:07:02.070
capable of aggregating about
1.5 trillion events every day.

00:07:02.070 --> 00:07:06.740
That's about one billion
events every minute.

00:07:06.740 --> 00:07:07.680
SAMEER FAROOQUI: Wow.

00:07:07.680 --> 00:07:09.880
Yeah, this is a
massive migration.

00:07:09.880 --> 00:07:12.130
Vrushali, can you talk a
bit about which teams were

00:07:12.130 --> 00:07:15.107
involved to accomplish this?

00:07:15.107 --> 00:07:17.440
VRUSHALI CHANNAPATTAN: So for
the partly cloudy project,

00:07:17.440 --> 00:07:20.140
it was not just the
Hadoop team at Twitter.

00:07:20.140 --> 00:07:22.480
Pretty much all of the
teams in data infrastructure

00:07:22.480 --> 00:07:25.680
were involved-- teams
that own Scalding, Presto,

00:07:25.680 --> 00:07:29.950
the analytical data tools that
we have, all of the libraries.

00:07:29.950 --> 00:07:32.230
And then it wasn't just
data infrastructure.

00:07:32.230 --> 00:07:34.060
We actually worked
with several teams

00:07:34.060 --> 00:07:35.920
at Twitter, like the
network engineering

00:07:35.920 --> 00:07:39.460
team, the public cloud services
team, the platform security

00:07:39.460 --> 00:07:40.660
team.

00:07:40.660 --> 00:07:43.790
And then it was not just
Twitter going at this all alone.

00:07:43.790 --> 00:07:46.780
We actually worked very
closely with Google on this.

00:07:46.780 --> 00:07:50.470
We in fact have an on site
Google presence at Twitter.

00:07:50.470 --> 00:07:53.410
So we have people from the
Google Professional Services

00:07:53.410 --> 00:07:55.960
Organization who are
on site at Twitter

00:07:55.960 --> 00:07:57.168
working very closely with us.

00:07:57.168 --> 00:07:58.793
SAMEER FAROOQUI: I
mean, Joep, you guys

00:07:58.793 --> 00:08:01.030
seem like a very
competent Hadoop group.

00:08:01.030 --> 00:08:03.850
Why did you decide to engage
Google Cloud Professional

00:08:03.850 --> 00:08:06.062
Services on this engagement?

00:08:06.062 --> 00:08:07.520
JOEP ROTTINGHUIS:
Well, we know how

00:08:07.520 --> 00:08:09.247
to run Hadoop
clusters on premise.

00:08:09.247 --> 00:08:11.330
You can imagine that, as
many companies have done,

00:08:11.330 --> 00:08:13.130
over the past decade, we've
built a lot of plumbing

00:08:13.130 --> 00:08:13.922
and infrastructure.

00:08:13.922 --> 00:08:17.450
And we-- it's like the larger
Twitter engineering work.

00:08:17.450 --> 00:08:20.480
So from how we build
services, how we deploy them,

00:08:20.480 --> 00:08:23.180
how we have configuration,
how we do monitoring,

00:08:23.180 --> 00:08:26.150
how we do alerting, all of
that infrastructure is in place

00:08:26.150 --> 00:08:28.790
and is build out for the
infrastructure within our data

00:08:28.790 --> 00:08:29.780
centers itself.

00:08:29.780 --> 00:08:32.030
So we know how to run clusters
and how to run services

00:08:32.030 --> 00:08:33.500
in our own data centers.

00:08:33.500 --> 00:08:36.500
As we decided to expand
that out to Google Cloud,

00:08:36.500 --> 00:08:38.600
we decided to engage
with our Google partners

00:08:38.600 --> 00:08:41.200
to make sure that we have a
much higher chance of success

00:08:41.200 --> 00:08:43.400
in making this successful.

00:08:43.400 --> 00:08:46.160
And through this
partnership, Google folks

00:08:46.160 --> 00:08:48.180
have found out that some
of the Twitter cases

00:08:48.180 --> 00:08:50.430
are slightly different maybe
than what you're used to.

00:08:50.430 --> 00:08:53.330
And we've made improvements
alongside with you guys.

00:08:53.330 --> 00:08:55.460
And on our side, we're
trying to adjust,

00:08:55.460 --> 00:08:58.370
make sure that we do things
well on the cloud side.

00:08:58.370 --> 00:09:00.748
And for our users, we're
trying to make sure that things

00:09:00.748 --> 00:09:01.790
are as similar as we can.

00:09:01.790 --> 00:09:03.582
So they have the same
experience on premise

00:09:03.582 --> 00:09:05.142
as well as they have in cloud.

00:09:05.142 --> 00:09:06.350
SAMEER FAROOQUI: Interesting.

00:09:06.350 --> 00:09:08.058
And Vrushali, what
are we looking at here

00:09:08.058 --> 00:09:09.170
with the work streams?

00:09:09.170 --> 00:09:13.040
How did you guys decide to
organize the different work

00:09:13.040 --> 00:09:14.102
streams, I suppose?

00:09:14.102 --> 00:09:15.560
VRUSHALI CHANNAPATTAN:
Right, so we

00:09:15.560 --> 00:09:17.870
had this goal of
extending Twitter's data

00:09:17.870 --> 00:09:22.200
platform to a hybrid cloud
model, on premises and cloud.

00:09:22.200 --> 00:09:23.870
So in order to
reach this goal, we

00:09:23.870 --> 00:09:27.230
started by identifying
the top broad work stream

00:09:27.230 --> 00:09:29.990
areas that needed to
be done in order to get

00:09:29.990 --> 00:09:31.760
to this hybrid model.

00:09:31.760 --> 00:09:34.760
And as each work
stream began to evolve,

00:09:34.760 --> 00:09:36.950
we came up with milestones.

00:09:36.950 --> 00:09:38.630
And those milestones
brought forward

00:09:38.630 --> 00:09:40.520
the dependencies
that existed between

00:09:40.520 --> 00:09:42.650
the different components
and services that

00:09:42.650 --> 00:09:46.290
needed to be completed in
order to reach that milestone.

00:09:46.290 --> 00:09:48.930
And pretty much, as we
kept working on this,

00:09:48.930 --> 00:09:51.650
we realized that we needed
a very close collaboration

00:09:51.650 --> 00:09:54.980
between all of the teams, not
just within Twitter, but also

00:09:54.980 --> 00:09:58.310
with Google to make this
adoption of GCP at Twitter

00:09:58.310 --> 00:09:59.390
successful.

00:09:59.390 --> 00:10:01.473
SAMEER FAROOQUI: So you
used an interesting word--

00:10:01.473 --> 00:10:04.220
you said extending your data
architecture to the cloud.

00:10:04.220 --> 00:10:07.430
Does that mean you're
keeping the on prem clusters

00:10:07.430 --> 00:10:10.190
and extending the
feature set to the cloud,

00:10:10.190 --> 00:10:13.850
or are you fully migrating
certain Hadoop clusters

00:10:13.850 --> 00:10:15.380
to the cloud?

00:10:15.380 --> 00:10:17.130
VRUSHALI CHANNAPATTAN:
So we are migrating

00:10:17.130 --> 00:10:20.670
our ad hoc and our cold
data storage to GCP.

00:10:20.670 --> 00:10:22.860
We are still keeping
our production clusters

00:10:22.860 --> 00:10:24.060
on premises.

00:10:24.060 --> 00:10:27.840
So some of the clusters will
continue to remain on premises.

00:10:27.840 --> 00:10:30.750
And we will have new clusters,
like ad hoc clusters,

00:10:30.750 --> 00:10:32.342
that will come up in GCP.

00:10:32.342 --> 00:10:33.300
SAMEER FAROOQUI: I see.

00:10:33.300 --> 00:10:34.470
So here we're looking--

00:10:34.470 --> 00:10:35.160
JOEP ROTTINGHUIS:
During the transition,

00:10:35.160 --> 00:10:37.560
there will be clusters in
both on premise and in cloud.

00:10:37.560 --> 00:10:39.780
So there will be a period
where both will be live--

00:10:39.780 --> 00:10:40.260
SAMEER FAROOQUI:
They'll overlap.

00:10:40.260 --> 00:10:42.750
JOEP ROTTINGHUIS: --until the
migration is fully complete.

00:10:42.750 --> 00:10:45.612
And at that point, we'll have
ad hoc clusters only in cloud.

00:10:45.612 --> 00:10:46.820
SAMEER FAROOQUI: Interesting.

00:10:46.820 --> 00:10:48.612
Joep, can you walk us
through this diagram?

00:10:48.612 --> 00:10:50.820
We're looking at the partly
cloudy architecture here.

00:10:50.820 --> 00:10:52.904
JOEP ROTTINGHUIS: Yeah,
what we're looking at here

00:10:52.904 --> 00:10:54.932
is a little bit of our
target architecture.

00:10:54.932 --> 00:10:57.390
As represented by the Twitter
birds on your left hand side,

00:10:57.390 --> 00:10:58.920
that's all of our
users interacting

00:10:58.920 --> 00:11:00.670
with the different systems.

00:11:00.670 --> 00:11:03.990
As Lohit said, these users
generate all these events

00:11:03.990 --> 00:11:08.500
that led in through our log
pipeline onto our RT clusters.

00:11:08.500 --> 00:11:10.327
That's what we call
the real time clusters.

00:11:10.327 --> 00:11:12.660
Those clusters are also used
to serve some outgoing data

00:11:12.660 --> 00:11:14.850
sets into production servers.

00:11:14.850 --> 00:11:17.100
And then that data is collected
through a log pipeline

00:11:17.100 --> 00:11:19.710
and goes into
production servers.

00:11:19.710 --> 00:11:21.720
This picture shows that
our ad hoc clusters

00:11:21.720 --> 00:11:24.630
are fully migrating into cloud
is the target architecture.

00:11:24.630 --> 00:11:27.630
And what we've done here is that
we're separating our storage

00:11:27.630 --> 00:11:30.330
into GCS so that all of
the files and objects

00:11:30.330 --> 00:11:32.880
are going to be stored in GCS.

00:11:32.880 --> 00:11:36.390
And the analysis is going to be
running on both Hadoop clusters

00:11:36.390 --> 00:11:37.890
as well as other tools.

00:11:37.890 --> 00:11:40.230
We will have Presto
and other tools

00:11:40.230 --> 00:11:41.947
that we will use, as well.

00:11:41.947 --> 00:11:43.530
SAMEER FAROOQUI: So
the ad hoc cluster

00:11:43.530 --> 00:11:47.430
has a Google compute
engine logo next to it.

00:11:47.430 --> 00:11:50.507
Is that what you're using
to run your ad hoc clusters?

00:11:50.507 --> 00:11:52.590
JOEP ROTTINGHUIS: Yeah,
the ad hoc clusters itself

00:11:52.590 --> 00:11:54.257
will be large shared
clusters that we're

00:11:54.257 --> 00:11:56.730
running on compute instances.

00:11:56.730 --> 00:11:58.358
There's other cases
where we Dataproc.

00:11:58.358 --> 00:11:59.400
That's underneath Presto.

00:11:59.400 --> 00:12:00.220
That's an example.

00:12:00.220 --> 00:12:02.250
There's other examples
where we spin up

00:12:02.250 --> 00:12:05.080
temporary Dataproc clusters
to load data into BigQuery,

00:12:05.080 --> 00:12:05.717
for example.

00:12:05.717 --> 00:12:07.550
So there's other cases
where those are used.

00:12:07.550 --> 00:12:09.030
SAMEER FAROOQUI: I'm curious
to dig into that a little bit

00:12:09.030 --> 00:12:09.570
more.

00:12:09.570 --> 00:12:13.410
How did you guys decide to
move your stack from on prem,

00:12:13.410 --> 00:12:16.350
as it is, I suppose, to
Google Cloud as opposed

00:12:16.350 --> 00:12:19.400
to using a managed
service like Dataproc?

00:12:19.400 --> 00:12:21.150
JOEP ROTTINGHUIS: Yeah,
there's the choice

00:12:21.150 --> 00:12:23.608
to either have all of Dataproc
have everything as a managed

00:12:23.608 --> 00:12:24.450
service.

00:12:24.450 --> 00:12:27.750
You can imagine when you
run a handful of jobs

00:12:27.750 --> 00:12:30.360
in a day then using
a cluster per job

00:12:30.360 --> 00:12:32.400
is a totally
appropriate situation.

00:12:32.400 --> 00:12:34.080
At our scale, when
we're running 80,000,

00:12:34.080 --> 00:12:36.390
100,000, 150,000
jobs in a day, if you

00:12:36.390 --> 00:12:38.880
were to launch 150,000
clusters in a day and then

00:12:38.880 --> 00:12:41.040
have to keep track of
who run what where,

00:12:41.040 --> 00:12:42.863
that becomes very
difficult to do.

00:12:42.863 --> 00:12:44.280
At the same time,
it was also very

00:12:44.280 --> 00:12:46.650
difficult to judge how big
that cluster needs to be.

00:12:46.650 --> 00:12:48.690
So if there's an
ad hoc case, users

00:12:48.690 --> 00:12:50.207
don't necessarily
know ahead of time

00:12:50.207 --> 00:12:51.540
how big the cluster needs to be.

00:12:51.540 --> 00:12:55.980
So if everybody were to
launch a 2,000 node cluster,

00:12:55.980 --> 00:12:57.480
then that would be
very inefficient.

00:12:57.480 --> 00:13:00.412
It would be very low
utilization at that point.

00:13:00.412 --> 00:13:02.870
That's the reason why we're
running larger shared clusters.

00:13:02.870 --> 00:13:04.410
SAMEER FAROOQUI: I see,
multi-tenant clusters.

00:13:04.410 --> 00:13:06.077
Yeah, I think that's
a really fair point

00:13:06.077 --> 00:13:07.800
you're making about Dataproc.

00:13:07.800 --> 00:13:12.060
Actually, last week we
released the GCP Broker service

00:13:12.060 --> 00:13:15.095
to actually address an
integration of Dataproc

00:13:15.095 --> 00:13:16.470
with curb rows
where you can have

00:13:16.470 --> 00:13:19.960
multi users within a
Dataproc cluster, as well.

00:13:19.960 --> 00:13:23.670
I'm also curious about the
Google Cloud Storage, Lohit.

00:13:23.670 --> 00:13:27.785
What kind of buckets are
you guys using for GCS?

00:13:27.785 --> 00:13:29.410
LOHIT VIJAYARENU: So
for the most part,

00:13:29.410 --> 00:13:33.840
we are using something called
as a dual region bucket where

00:13:33.840 --> 00:13:35.850
if we load the data into
those buckets from two

00:13:35.850 --> 00:13:38.100
of the regions, the
data will automatically

00:13:38.100 --> 00:13:42.570
be replicated and consistently
be available from the two

00:13:42.570 --> 00:13:45.960
regions not spread
across the entire region

00:13:45.960 --> 00:13:48.232
like, for example, US
region, or something.

00:13:48.232 --> 00:13:49.190
SAMEER FAROOQUI: I see.

00:13:49.190 --> 00:13:49.850
JOEP ROTTINGHUIS:
It looks like Asheem

00:13:49.850 --> 00:13:51.770
has a question from Twitter.

00:13:51.770 --> 00:13:53.970
ASHEEM: Yeah,
there were a couple

00:13:53.970 --> 00:13:57.150
of questions around Dataproc,
which I think we answered.

00:13:57.150 --> 00:13:59.190
But one new question
that came up

00:13:59.190 --> 00:14:02.430
was how are you thinking about
storing tweet impression data?

00:14:02.430 --> 00:14:05.670
What data models or
tools are you leveraging?

00:14:05.670 --> 00:14:09.495
This is from Kat on Twitter.

00:14:09.495 --> 00:14:10.870
JOEP ROTTINGHUIS:
So the question

00:14:10.870 --> 00:14:14.060
is what data models
are we using?

00:14:14.060 --> 00:14:15.810
ASHEEM: Yeah, so what
data models or tools

00:14:15.810 --> 00:14:16.920
are you leveraging?

00:14:16.920 --> 00:14:18.420
JOEP ROTTINGHUIS:
So from our change

00:14:18.420 --> 00:14:20.478
from on premise to
cloud, our users

00:14:20.478 --> 00:14:22.020
are going to still
use the same tools

00:14:22.020 --> 00:14:23.145
that they're using on prem.

00:14:23.145 --> 00:14:25.200
So the change from
on premise to cloud

00:14:25.200 --> 00:14:27.810
is not going to change the
format or the storage of how

00:14:27.810 --> 00:14:29.550
tweets or our data is stored.

00:14:29.550 --> 00:14:31.140
That remains the same.

00:14:31.140 --> 00:14:35.030
LOHIT VIJAYARENU: Yeah, so to
add to that, most of the data--

00:14:35.030 --> 00:14:38.280
like so if a service,
a new microservice

00:14:38.280 --> 00:14:41.730
is emitting some
events, they have a well

00:14:41.730 --> 00:14:43.650
defined schema
associated with that.

00:14:43.650 --> 00:14:47.190
And for the most part,
it's like a Thrift schema.

00:14:47.190 --> 00:14:49.080
And then we compress
it using LZO.

00:14:49.080 --> 00:14:53.240
So it's like a LZO Thrift
format as Vrushali told you.

00:14:53.240 --> 00:14:55.670
SAMEER FAROOQUI: And Lohit,
talking more about disks,

00:14:55.670 --> 00:14:59.210
are you using persistent disks,
as well, on the ad hoc cluster?

00:14:59.210 --> 00:15:02.577
Because I can see Google
Cloud Storage there.

00:15:02.577 --> 00:15:04.410
LOHIT VIJAYARENU: So
yes, it's a combination

00:15:04.410 --> 00:15:07.230
of a persistent disk and GCS.

00:15:07.230 --> 00:15:08.640
So what we are--

00:15:08.640 --> 00:15:11.070
first, when we
started to evaluate,

00:15:11.070 --> 00:15:12.690
we thought about
options of running

00:15:12.690 --> 00:15:17.550
just HDFS on those VMs, which
would be very inefficient.

00:15:17.550 --> 00:15:20.940
Because you need to replicate
like three ways for HDFS.

00:15:20.940 --> 00:15:24.370
So what we ended up doing was--

00:15:24.370 --> 00:15:27.540
in a MapReduce, generally
typically in an analytics

00:15:27.540 --> 00:15:29.970
pipeline, a MapReduce
has multiple stages

00:15:29.970 --> 00:15:33.600
where output of one stage
gets returned to disk.

00:15:33.600 --> 00:15:36.840
And the next stage reads that
data, does some processing,

00:15:36.840 --> 00:15:38.580
and then writes a disk again.

00:15:38.580 --> 00:15:42.030
So for the intermediate
stages for fast access

00:15:42.030 --> 00:15:44.760
and temporary data we are
using persistent disk.

00:15:44.760 --> 00:15:49.710
But the initial input and
the final output is on GCS.

00:15:49.710 --> 00:15:51.960
SAMEER FAROOQUI: I see, so
the source of truth is GCS.

00:15:51.960 --> 00:15:55.440
But you use the persistent
disk as shuffle space

00:15:55.440 --> 00:15:57.530
or intermediate
job storage space.

00:15:57.530 --> 00:15:58.050
JOEP ROTTINGHUIS: And
the advantage of that,

00:15:58.050 --> 00:16:00.600
as well, is that we can
now use other cloud native

00:16:00.600 --> 00:16:02.070
tools to leverage that.

00:16:02.070 --> 00:16:04.260
So if people want to use
Dataproc or in some cases

00:16:04.260 --> 00:16:06.622
BigQuery, they can use
directly the data from GCS.

00:16:06.622 --> 00:16:08.580
And we don't have to
store that multiple times.

00:16:08.580 --> 00:16:09.780
SAMEER FAROOQUI: I
see, so I definitely

00:16:09.780 --> 00:16:11.500
want to dig a little bit
more into the details here.

00:16:11.500 --> 00:16:13.560
But before we move on,
can you talk a little bit

00:16:13.560 --> 00:16:15.240
about the log pipeline, Lohit?

00:16:15.240 --> 00:16:16.650
What exactly is happening there?

00:16:16.650 --> 00:16:18.780
And you're writing to
real time clusters.

00:16:18.780 --> 00:16:20.790
And then it goes to
a production cluster.

00:16:20.790 --> 00:16:23.460
What exactly does that
pipeline look like?

00:16:23.460 --> 00:16:26.210
LOHIT VIJAYARENU: So as I
said in the very beginning,

00:16:26.210 --> 00:16:31.810
the actions, users actions,
generate a lot of these events.

00:16:31.810 --> 00:16:34.290
So each of these
events has a name

00:16:34.290 --> 00:16:36.710
associated with it, which
ultimately translates

00:16:36.710 --> 00:16:38.340
to our data set name.

00:16:38.340 --> 00:16:41.580
So the log pipeline is
responsible for aggregating

00:16:41.580 --> 00:16:45.120
all the events which we
receive from thousands

00:16:45.120 --> 00:16:48.660
of different servers,
aggregate all of them,

00:16:48.660 --> 00:16:52.410
and then write it as log
files on HDFS, usually time

00:16:52.410 --> 00:16:52.920
partitioned.

00:16:52.920 --> 00:16:55.950
We do it on an hourly boundary.

00:16:55.950 --> 00:16:59.100
We also do some cleansing,
format conversion,

00:16:59.100 --> 00:17:00.870
depending on the data set.

00:17:00.870 --> 00:17:03.520
And then we have a
replication framework,

00:17:03.520 --> 00:17:06.480
which takes care of copying
the data from real time cluster

00:17:06.480 --> 00:17:09.210
to production or other places.

00:17:09.210 --> 00:17:11.310
I also do want to
mention that we call it

00:17:11.310 --> 00:17:14.099
real time because those
are the clusters which

00:17:14.099 --> 00:17:16.230
are the incoming clusters.

00:17:16.230 --> 00:17:19.260
The uptime on those clusters
are like several years.

00:17:19.260 --> 00:17:21.277
We rarely take
down that cluster.

00:17:21.277 --> 00:17:23.069
SAMEER FAROOQUI: Do
you run any Hadoop jobs

00:17:23.069 --> 00:17:24.810
on those real time clusters?

00:17:24.810 --> 00:17:26.910
LOHIT VIJAYARENU: We
do not run Hadoop jobs,

00:17:26.910 --> 00:17:28.200
mainly because of this reason.

00:17:28.200 --> 00:17:31.620
Because Hadoop jobs
can be unpredictable,

00:17:31.620 --> 00:17:33.090
depending on user actions.

00:17:33.090 --> 00:17:37.110
So it's mostly these clusters
are incoming clusters

00:17:37.110 --> 00:17:40.680
to get the data and then serve
some subset of data sets.

00:17:40.680 --> 00:17:42.930
SAMEER FAROOQUI: So you
mentioned the data replicator.

00:17:42.930 --> 00:17:44.460
I'm assuming the
data replicator is

00:17:44.460 --> 00:17:47.640
moving the data from
these on premise

00:17:47.640 --> 00:17:49.482
clusters between each other.

00:17:49.482 --> 00:17:50.940
Can you talk a
little bit about how

00:17:50.940 --> 00:17:53.700
it works when you use the
data replicator to move data

00:17:53.700 --> 00:17:54.870
to the cloud?

00:17:54.870 --> 00:17:56.070
LOHIT VIJAYARENU: Sure.

00:17:56.070 --> 00:17:59.330
We already have a well defined
service data applicator

00:17:59.330 --> 00:18:03.370
with given a source, and a
destination, and a data set,

00:18:03.370 --> 00:18:06.390
it can keep track of
changes at the source

00:18:06.390 --> 00:18:09.690
and make sure all the data
is synced to the destination.

00:18:09.690 --> 00:18:12.090
Whenever new data
sets arrive in source,

00:18:12.090 --> 00:18:14.580
it will make sure that
that data is replicated.

00:18:14.580 --> 00:18:19.320
So for GCS, what we
did was we created

00:18:19.320 --> 00:18:21.270
something called as
a copy cluster, which

00:18:21.270 --> 00:18:24.570
is like an in
transit cluster which

00:18:24.570 --> 00:18:28.320
has data connectivity
to the Google endpoints.

00:18:28.320 --> 00:18:30.270
And we set up a
similar replicator

00:18:30.270 --> 00:18:34.900
like how we have for on prem
for cloud also and then said,

00:18:34.900 --> 00:18:37.170
OK, sources are
input, HDFS cluster,

00:18:37.170 --> 00:18:39.508
and destination is GCS.

00:18:39.508 --> 00:18:41.050
SAMEER FAROOQUI:
This is interesting.

00:18:41.050 --> 00:18:43.350
I mean, for the audience
members who might not

00:18:43.350 --> 00:18:46.050
be familiar with
DistCp, can you explain

00:18:46.050 --> 00:18:47.490
what distributed copy is?

00:18:47.490 --> 00:18:51.170
And I'm kind of curious about
why the data-- is the data

00:18:51.170 --> 00:18:53.640
replicator just kind of
kicking off DistCp jobs?

00:18:53.640 --> 00:18:57.232
Or what is it
exactly doing there?

00:18:57.232 --> 00:18:58.190
LOHIT VIJAYARENU: Sure.

00:18:58.190 --> 00:19:01.270
So as I said, for
a particular data

00:19:01.270 --> 00:19:03.540
set we have a well
defined partitions,

00:19:03.540 --> 00:19:05.400
in this case, hourly partitions.

00:19:05.400 --> 00:19:07.990
SAMEER FAROOQUI: I see that, and
the year, month, day, hour, I

00:19:07.990 --> 00:19:08.490
guess.

00:19:08.490 --> 00:19:11.280
LOHIT VIJAYARENU: Hour, right,
for this data set on ClusterX.

00:19:11.280 --> 00:19:14.040
So what the data
replicator does here

00:19:14.040 --> 00:19:19.110
is that for every new hourly
partition it sees at the source

00:19:19.110 --> 00:19:22.110
it kicks off one DistCp job.

00:19:22.110 --> 00:19:27.060
So the DistCp job is our
standard MapReduce application.

00:19:27.060 --> 00:19:30.750
It basically copies a
lot of files in parallel,

00:19:30.750 --> 00:19:33.060
taking advantage of the
parallelism of a Hadoop

00:19:33.060 --> 00:19:33.940
cluster.

00:19:33.940 --> 00:19:36.240
So, if you want to
make it go fast,

00:19:36.240 --> 00:19:38.010
you just throw in more mappers.

00:19:38.010 --> 00:19:41.190
And then it can copy the data.

00:19:41.190 --> 00:19:44.670
So that's the DistCp job,
which is reading the data.

00:19:44.670 --> 00:19:47.793
And, here, you can see
that the copy cluster,

00:19:47.793 --> 00:19:49.210
even though it's
a Hadoop cluster,

00:19:49.210 --> 00:19:51.660
it never saves any data locally.

00:19:51.660 --> 00:19:55.500
It's not stored in the copy
cluster and then copied to GCS.

00:19:55.500 --> 00:19:58.110
It's a pass through DistCp.

00:19:58.110 --> 00:20:00.570
SAMEER FAROOQUI: I see,
and Joep, what is that--

00:20:00.570 --> 00:20:02.730
sorry-- oh, Asheem, go ahead.

00:20:02.730 --> 00:20:05.530
ASHEEM: So there was another
question around security,

00:20:05.530 --> 00:20:09.810
but I think we can tackle this
on a Presto question, which

00:20:09.810 --> 00:20:11.070
is similar.

00:20:11.070 --> 00:20:14.100
Did you have to make any changes
for Presto to work on GCP?

00:20:16.567 --> 00:20:18.150
VRUSHALI CHANNAPATTAN:
So yes, I think

00:20:18.150 --> 00:20:19.800
that's on the previous slide.

00:20:19.800 --> 00:20:23.440
So we are running some Presto
clusters in GCP, as well.

00:20:23.440 --> 00:20:25.770
We had to make some
changes to Presto.

00:20:25.770 --> 00:20:28.710
And those changes were
basically required

00:20:28.710 --> 00:20:32.970
because we wanted to follow our
triple-a design principles, so

00:20:32.970 --> 00:20:38.060
triple-a meaning authentication,
authorization, and auditing.

00:20:38.060 --> 00:20:42.450
So authentication is when users
identify themselves as who

00:20:42.450 --> 00:20:43.330
they are.

00:20:43.330 --> 00:20:45.810
So if a user says-- let's
say I say I'm Vrushali.

00:20:45.810 --> 00:20:48.540
I need to identify myself, let's
say with my driver's license

00:20:48.540 --> 00:20:49.290
or something.

00:20:49.290 --> 00:20:51.870
I should be able
to say who I am.

00:20:51.870 --> 00:20:53.370
That is authentication.

00:20:53.370 --> 00:20:55.980
Authorization is when
we want to make sure

00:20:55.980 --> 00:20:58.650
that users can actually
access the data that they're

00:20:58.650 --> 00:20:59.820
trying to access.

00:20:59.820 --> 00:21:02.790
So are they authorized
to access that data?

00:21:02.790 --> 00:21:05.880
And auditing is when
we want to know who

00:21:05.880 --> 00:21:08.150
accessed which data set, when?

00:21:08.150 --> 00:21:10.290
And we wanted to
have a very clear cut

00:21:10.290 --> 00:21:12.240
way of determining that.

00:21:12.240 --> 00:21:15.180
So those three triple-a
design principles

00:21:15.180 --> 00:21:18.353
made us make some
changes to Presto.

00:21:18.353 --> 00:21:20.020
SAMEER FAROOQUI:
Interesting, and Presto

00:21:20.020 --> 00:21:21.450
has a Dataproc logo next to it.

00:21:21.450 --> 00:21:24.010
Is that what you guys are
using right now to run Presto?

00:21:24.010 --> 00:21:25.240
VRUSHALI CHANNAPATTAN:
Yes, that is correct.

00:21:25.240 --> 00:21:27.157
SAMEER FAROOQUI: OK, can
you talk a little bit

00:21:27.157 --> 00:21:29.500
about the scale that you guys
have benchmarked or tested

00:21:29.500 --> 00:21:32.850
Presto in Google Cloud?

00:21:32.850 --> 00:21:35.260
VRUSHALI CHANNAPATTAN: So
we've run Dataproc with about--

00:21:35.260 --> 00:21:39.700
Presto with about 90,000 cores.

00:21:39.700 --> 00:21:41.200
SAMEER FAROOQUI:
That's interesting,

00:21:41.200 --> 00:21:43.980
because I think that's the
largest Dataproc cluster that

00:21:43.980 --> 00:21:48.550
has ever been launched, as far
as I know for public customers.

00:21:48.550 --> 00:21:49.900
Before we move on into a--

00:21:49.900 --> 00:21:51.192
JOEP ROTTINGHUIS: Hold my beer.

00:21:51.192 --> 00:21:52.775
We'll launch more
large clusters soon.

00:21:52.775 --> 00:21:54.150
SAMEER FAROOQUI:
OK, well, you'll

00:21:54.150 --> 00:21:56.110
beat your own record
very soon, then.

00:21:56.110 --> 00:21:59.530
Before we move away from the
data replication slide, Lohit,

00:21:59.530 --> 00:22:01.660
I'm curious also about
the 800 gigabits.

00:22:01.660 --> 00:22:06.040
I mean, that's a very fat
network pipe to say the least.

00:22:06.040 --> 00:22:07.660
How is that network configured?

00:22:07.660 --> 00:22:09.790
How do you handle
security around that?

00:22:09.790 --> 00:22:10.473
I'm curious.

00:22:10.473 --> 00:22:11.890
LOHIT VIJAYARENU:
Yeah, so when we

00:22:11.890 --> 00:22:16.000
decided to set up these
copy replication from our

00:22:16.000 --> 00:22:24.470
on prem Hadoop clusters to GCS,
what we decided was the data--

00:22:24.470 --> 00:22:26.510
there are some
metadata operations,

00:22:26.510 --> 00:22:29.710
which the data replicator
or services has

00:22:29.710 --> 00:22:33.820
to do to say look up the source
and destination directory,

00:22:33.820 --> 00:22:35.860
make sure all the
files are there or not.

00:22:35.860 --> 00:22:37.660
Those are metadata operations.

00:22:37.660 --> 00:22:40.360
But the actual bulk
transfer, the data transfer

00:22:40.360 --> 00:22:43.310
would be in the order
of petabytes of data.

00:22:43.310 --> 00:22:46.780
So our network engineers,
with the Google folks,

00:22:46.780 --> 00:22:51.260
what they ended up doing
is to do a Twitter,

00:22:51.260 --> 00:22:56.770
Google PNI set up, a private
interconnect between us

00:22:56.770 --> 00:23:00.740
and will provide a 800
gigabits per second bandwidth.

00:23:00.740 --> 00:23:03.850
Now all the nodes
in the copy cluster

00:23:03.850 --> 00:23:07.240
have direct access
to the GCS endpoints.

00:23:07.240 --> 00:23:09.598
SAMEER FAROOQUI: Is that
what the orange thing is?

00:23:09.598 --> 00:23:11.140
LOHIT VIJAYARENU:
So the orange thing

00:23:11.140 --> 00:23:13.660
is very interesting stuff.

00:23:13.660 --> 00:23:16.690
That is the Google
Hadoop connector.

00:23:16.690 --> 00:23:21.160
So Hadoop file system is
like an abstract system.

00:23:21.160 --> 00:23:24.010
HDFS is, in fact,
just an implementation

00:23:24.010 --> 00:23:25.760
of that interface.

00:23:25.760 --> 00:23:27.250
So what Google
ended up doing was

00:23:27.250 --> 00:23:29.960
they implemented
the same interface.

00:23:29.960 --> 00:23:34.690
So any API file system API
calls would be automatically

00:23:34.690 --> 00:23:38.050
translated to the Google
Cloud Storage APIs

00:23:38.050 --> 00:23:38.980
using that connector.

00:23:38.980 --> 00:23:40.900
It's like a library.

00:23:40.900 --> 00:23:43.780
So the advantage now
is that any application

00:23:43.780 --> 00:23:47.450
which is using the Hadoop APIs
don't need to change anything.

00:23:47.450 --> 00:23:50.410
So they can interact
with Google Cloud Storage

00:23:50.410 --> 00:23:52.810
as if it's like any
other Hadoop storage.

00:23:52.810 --> 00:23:56.470
So for the most part, we didn't
have to make any code changes

00:23:56.470 --> 00:23:58.030
in the data replicator.

00:23:58.030 --> 00:23:59.150
So we just plugged it in.

00:23:59.150 --> 00:24:01.913
And then GCP would just work.

00:24:01.913 --> 00:24:02.830
SAMEER FAROOQUI: Sure.

00:24:02.830 --> 00:24:05.500
I definitely want to come back
to the GCS connector again.

00:24:05.500 --> 00:24:07.958
I know that there were some
really interesting developments

00:24:07.958 --> 00:24:11.960
that happened in GCS connector
because of the Twitter project.

00:24:11.960 --> 00:24:14.460
But before I move away from
this slide, Joep, I'm curious.

00:24:14.460 --> 00:24:17.260
800 gigabits, that's a fat
pipe, like I said before.

00:24:17.260 --> 00:24:18.455
So that means--

00:24:18.455 --> 00:24:19.830
I just did math
before this talk.

00:24:19.830 --> 00:24:23.050
One petabyte you can move
in just under three hours.

00:24:23.050 --> 00:24:26.470
So 300 petabytes
would be about 35 days

00:24:26.470 --> 00:24:28.810
if you have the pipe
running at full stream.

00:24:28.810 --> 00:24:31.420
With that in mind, how long
is this data migration project

00:24:31.420 --> 00:24:32.008
taking?

00:24:32.008 --> 00:24:34.050
JOEP ROTTINGHUIS: Yeah,
we've done some modeling.

00:24:34.050 --> 00:24:36.347
And if you were to go full
bore and pump it across,

00:24:36.347 --> 00:24:37.680
it would take about four months.

00:24:37.680 --> 00:24:40.920
Keep in mind that this data is
not a steady data set, either.

00:24:40.920 --> 00:24:43.950
New pieces of information
keep coming in.

00:24:43.950 --> 00:24:46.902
And in the days of privacy
and data protection and GDPR

00:24:46.902 --> 00:24:48.735
regulations, some of
these data sets of ours

00:24:48.735 --> 00:24:49.620
are getting scrubbed.

00:24:49.620 --> 00:24:51.610
So it used to be
that, for example,

00:24:51.610 --> 00:24:53.670
an hour worth of
tweets was written once

00:24:53.670 --> 00:24:55.440
and would never be changed.

00:24:55.440 --> 00:24:57.420
We would consider it to
be very cold data sets.

00:24:57.420 --> 00:25:00.270
Their written once, and only
the new hours are coming in.

00:25:00.270 --> 00:25:01.895
As you have a right
to be forgotten

00:25:01.895 --> 00:25:03.270
and those kind of
functionalities

00:25:03.270 --> 00:25:05.270
have to be implemented,
some of these partitions

00:25:05.270 --> 00:25:06.360
actually get rewritten.

00:25:06.360 --> 00:25:09.030
So as the time goes by,
as you're copying data,

00:25:09.030 --> 00:25:11.100
and as it progressing,
some of this data

00:25:11.100 --> 00:25:12.592
is changing and is modifying.

00:25:12.592 --> 00:25:14.550
So the way we're setting
this up and the reason

00:25:14.550 --> 00:25:16.560
why we have a copy cluster
is that that is also

00:25:16.560 --> 00:25:20.340
providing some funneling where
we can do prioritization.

00:25:20.340 --> 00:25:22.170
The latest hour can
be copied first.

00:25:22.170 --> 00:25:25.140
And then in the valley
of-- during the peak,

00:25:25.140 --> 00:25:27.430
you have the new hour
of data that comes in.

00:25:27.430 --> 00:25:29.430
And then in the valley,
when the network traffic

00:25:29.430 --> 00:25:32.582
is less utilized, we can
do the backfill part.

00:25:32.582 --> 00:25:33.540
SAMEER FAROOQUI: I say.

00:25:33.540 --> 00:25:35.123
Vrushali, can you
give us just a sense

00:25:35.123 --> 00:25:37.890
of how many data sets
are involved that you

00:25:37.890 --> 00:25:40.050
have on premise at Twitter?

00:25:40.050 --> 00:25:45.285
And is this all moving to
one massive GCS bucket?

00:25:45.285 --> 00:25:46.660
VRUSHALI CHANNAPATTAN:
So when we

00:25:46.660 --> 00:25:52.120
were thinking of how to move
this data set on HDFS into GCS,

00:25:52.120 --> 00:25:54.100
we had a few different options.

00:25:54.100 --> 00:25:56.650
Either we could just put
everything in one bucket

00:25:56.650 --> 00:25:58.960
and have everybody access it.

00:25:58.960 --> 00:26:00.970
But then that doesn't
sound very good, right?

00:26:00.970 --> 00:26:04.110
So we had the other option of
trying to group together data

00:26:04.110 --> 00:26:07.570
sets and put them
in some buckets.

00:26:07.570 --> 00:26:09.010
But then the model
that we picked

00:26:09.010 --> 00:26:11.860
was going with the data
set ownership model.

00:26:11.860 --> 00:26:13.540
So every data set owner--

00:26:13.540 --> 00:26:16.180
per data set owner,
we create one bucket.

00:26:16.180 --> 00:26:18.253
And that is what
we ended up with.

00:26:18.253 --> 00:26:20.170
SAMEER FAROOQUI: How
many data sets are there?

00:26:20.170 --> 00:26:22.330
VRUSHALI CHANNAPATTAN:
About hundreds of data sets.

00:26:22.330 --> 00:26:24.850
Close to thousand-- I
mean, 1,000, I would say.

00:26:24.850 --> 00:26:28.210
SAMEER FAROOQUI: And how do
you organize your architecture

00:26:28.210 --> 00:26:30.118
within Google Cloud?

00:26:30.118 --> 00:26:32.410
VRUSHALI CHANNAPATTAN: So
for organizing these buckets,

00:26:32.410 --> 00:26:35.530
we wanted to think about how we
want to put them in projects.

00:26:35.530 --> 00:26:37.630
Either we could put them
again in one project

00:26:37.630 --> 00:26:40.850
and have everybody just run
all of their resources there.

00:26:40.850 --> 00:26:44.530
But then at our scale, that
becomes too big and unwieldy.

00:26:44.530 --> 00:26:47.950
So then we thought about
going the other extreme that

00:26:47.950 --> 00:26:51.770
would be having one project
per small application.

00:26:51.770 --> 00:26:55.100
But again, at our scale, that
becomes too big to manage.

00:26:55.100 --> 00:26:57.070
So we picked a middle ground.

00:26:57.070 --> 00:27:01.210
And we said, OK, let's have a
one project per business unit.

00:27:01.210 --> 00:27:03.460
So for example, for Twitter
infrastructure engineering

00:27:03.460 --> 00:27:05.020
we have one GCP project.

00:27:05.020 --> 00:27:06.730
For Twitter revenue,
we have one.

00:27:06.730 --> 00:27:09.040
So that way we have a
few handful of projects

00:27:09.040 --> 00:27:10.420
that we can manage.

00:27:10.420 --> 00:27:13.880
And then we organize our
buckets within those projects.

00:27:13.880 --> 00:27:16.360
So having a few
projects does give us

00:27:16.360 --> 00:27:19.240
some delineation, some
boundaries between what we want

00:27:19.240 --> 00:27:21.400
to do across these projects.

00:27:21.400 --> 00:27:23.980
But then we have a few
of them to manage--

00:27:23.980 --> 00:27:25.703
not too many or just one.

00:27:25.703 --> 00:27:27.370
SAMEER FAROOQUI: Sure,
I mean-- so Joep,

00:27:27.370 --> 00:27:29.150
I understand the scope
of these projects

00:27:29.150 --> 00:27:32.650
are just like billing scopes
and perhaps security scopes.

00:27:32.650 --> 00:27:34.600
But what exactly is
in these projects?

00:27:34.600 --> 00:27:37.450
I see a Hadoop logo
and a GCS bucket?

00:27:37.450 --> 00:27:40.190
So how many Hadoop clusters are
you running in these projects?

00:27:40.190 --> 00:27:43.427
And how many GCS buckets
are on these projects?

00:27:43.427 --> 00:27:45.010
JOEP ROTTINGHUIS:
Yeah, like you said,

00:27:45.010 --> 00:27:48.360
the project is a delineation
and there's a security boundary,

00:27:48.360 --> 00:27:48.860
as well.

00:27:48.860 --> 00:27:51.193
So within each project, we're
running one Hadoop cluster

00:27:51.193 --> 00:27:52.400
that is shared.

00:27:52.400 --> 00:27:54.850
And this is, again, done
for fragmentation purposes.

00:27:54.850 --> 00:27:56.803
If you had one very
large cluster, that

00:27:56.803 --> 00:27:58.720
would be great from
fragmentation perspective,

00:27:58.720 --> 00:28:01.278
because all the users bin
pack very well together.

00:28:01.278 --> 00:28:03.070
If you were to go to
the very other extreme

00:28:03.070 --> 00:28:04.823
where you run one
cluster per job,

00:28:04.823 --> 00:28:06.490
you have a huge amount
of fragmentation.

00:28:06.490 --> 00:28:08.480
And whenever the cluster
is not fully used,

00:28:08.480 --> 00:28:09.730
it's going to sit around idle.

00:28:09.730 --> 00:28:11.605
So we chose the middle
ground where we end up

00:28:11.605 --> 00:28:13.990
delineating, saying one cluster
for each of these pillar

00:28:13.990 --> 00:28:17.650
projects, or pillar
organizations, we call them.

00:28:17.650 --> 00:28:19.840
And then all of the
buckets that belong

00:28:19.840 --> 00:28:22.900
to all of the users that are
in that pillar organization

00:28:22.900 --> 00:28:24.220
fall along those lines.

00:28:24.220 --> 00:28:26.840
So there will be a set
of buckets for each user.

00:28:26.840 --> 00:28:29.260
There will be a set of
buckets for each log category.

00:28:29.260 --> 00:28:31.970
And to clarify, when
we think of data sets,

00:28:31.970 --> 00:28:35.440
there's roughly two sets of data
sets that we're thinking of.

00:28:35.440 --> 00:28:37.340
You have the initial
log categories.

00:28:37.340 --> 00:28:39.550
And that's an order
of magnitude of 1,000.

00:28:39.550 --> 00:28:41.875
And then you have about
1,000 individual users,

00:28:41.875 --> 00:28:43.000
order of magnitude of that.

00:28:43.000 --> 00:28:46.428
And they each have dozens
of their own data sets.

00:28:46.428 --> 00:28:48.470
So the total number of
data sets is fairly large.

00:28:48.470 --> 00:28:51.250
But if you think of the
incoming log categories

00:28:51.250 --> 00:28:54.800
that Lohit explained, that's
order of magnitude of 1,000.

00:28:54.800 --> 00:28:57.100
So it will be several
thousand buckets

00:28:57.100 --> 00:28:58.652
for each of these projects.

00:28:58.652 --> 00:28:59.860
SAMEER FAROOQUI: Interesting.

00:28:59.860 --> 00:29:04.510
So Vrushali, I want to
go back to the triple-a--

00:29:04.510 --> 00:29:07.170
I think you said authentication,
authorization, and auditing?

00:29:07.170 --> 00:29:08.920
VRUSHALI CHANNAPATTAN:
That is right, yes.

00:29:08.920 --> 00:29:09.940
SAMEER FAROOQUI: I
want to go back to that

00:29:09.940 --> 00:29:11.770
and think about
that a little bit.

00:29:11.770 --> 00:29:17.230
So authentication, I think
that's just a user says

00:29:17.230 --> 00:29:19.450
they are who they are.

00:29:19.450 --> 00:29:21.400
Authorization, you've
got to give them

00:29:21.400 --> 00:29:23.170
permission to do something.

00:29:23.170 --> 00:29:25.570
And finally, auditing--
can you talk a little bit

00:29:25.570 --> 00:29:29.260
about that in respect to how
you set up your security model

00:29:29.260 --> 00:29:31.420
and user permissions for Hadoop?

00:29:31.420 --> 00:29:33.340
VRUSHALI CHANNAPATTAN:
Right, so let's start

00:29:33.340 --> 00:29:35.830
by understanding how
users do their work

00:29:35.830 --> 00:29:38.440
on premises and in GCP.

00:29:38.440 --> 00:29:42.350
So we have users, that's
the blue figure there.

00:29:42.350 --> 00:29:45.040
So every user has a
Unix account that they

00:29:45.040 --> 00:29:48.640
use to run their day
to day on premise jobs,

00:29:48.640 --> 00:29:51.520
in cluster, whatever
work they need to do.

00:29:51.520 --> 00:29:55.030
Now this user also
has a G Suite account

00:29:55.030 --> 00:29:57.700
that they use for their
email, their Google Docs,

00:29:57.700 --> 00:30:00.050
Sheets, all of that work.

00:30:00.050 --> 00:30:04.120
Now, when we think
about a process running,

00:30:04.120 --> 00:30:06.640
the process runs as a Unix user.

00:30:06.640 --> 00:30:11.050
Now that process needs to
read data or write to GCS.

00:30:11.050 --> 00:30:13.390
Now in order to
do that, it needs

00:30:13.390 --> 00:30:15.820
to have the right permissions.

00:30:15.820 --> 00:30:20.200
One way access the data
in GCS is via G Suite.

00:30:20.200 --> 00:30:23.020
But then that means that you
need to provision the G Suite

00:30:23.020 --> 00:30:26.650
credentials on the VM node.

00:30:26.650 --> 00:30:28.060
We did not want to do that.

00:30:28.060 --> 00:30:28.725
So we pick--

00:30:28.725 --> 00:30:29.600
SAMEER FAROOQUI: Why?

00:30:29.600 --> 00:30:31.808
VRUSHALI CHANNAPATTAN: So
we do not want to provision

00:30:31.808 --> 00:30:33.970
the G Suite credentials
on a particular VM,

00:30:33.970 --> 00:30:36.940
because a person who has
admin privileges on the VM

00:30:36.940 --> 00:30:39.260
then can act as that user.

00:30:39.260 --> 00:30:42.440
So then that person would
have access to their G Suite--

00:30:42.440 --> 00:30:45.580
Google Docs, Gmail, all of that.

00:30:45.580 --> 00:30:48.800
So on the VMs, we provision
a shadow account--

00:30:48.800 --> 00:30:50.780
what we call as a
shadow account--

00:30:50.780 --> 00:30:52.630
per user.

00:30:52.630 --> 00:30:54.650
SAMEER FAROOQUI:
What is a shadowing?

00:30:54.650 --> 00:30:56.400
VRUSHALI CHANNAPATTAN:
So a shadow account

00:30:56.400 --> 00:31:00.850
is nothing but a Google service
account that exists in GCP.

00:31:00.850 --> 00:31:04.150
And there is one GCP
account, one GCP service

00:31:04.150 --> 00:31:06.640
account per Unix user.

00:31:06.640 --> 00:31:08.710
And a Google service
account has a set

00:31:08.710 --> 00:31:10.730
of keys associated with it.

00:31:10.730 --> 00:31:14.560
And those keys and that account
is provisioned on those VMs.

00:31:14.560 --> 00:31:17.800
So that way you only have
that account and the key

00:31:17.800 --> 00:31:20.280
that is existing on that node.

00:31:20.280 --> 00:31:22.840
But it is transparent
to the user themselves

00:31:22.840 --> 00:31:24.975
when they are running a job.

00:31:24.975 --> 00:31:27.350
JOEP ROTTINGHUIS: Think of
that as when you park your car

00:31:27.350 --> 00:31:29.270
and you come and you
have valet parking.

00:31:29.270 --> 00:31:32.090
When you hand over
your key, you may not

00:31:32.090 --> 00:31:34.250
want the person parking
your car to be able to get

00:31:34.250 --> 00:31:35.570
into your glove compartment.

00:31:35.570 --> 00:31:37.202
But this is actually
one step further.

00:31:37.202 --> 00:31:38.910
If you want to also
be able to say, well,

00:31:38.910 --> 00:31:40.993
this person can drive your
car and can drive slow,

00:31:40.993 --> 00:31:42.620
but they cannot
drive to the freeway.

00:31:42.620 --> 00:31:44.150
They can drive around, but
they cannot have a party

00:31:44.150 --> 00:31:44.650
in the back.

00:31:44.650 --> 00:31:45.603
That's not OK.

00:31:45.603 --> 00:31:47.270
So this, essentially,
is a different set

00:31:47.270 --> 00:31:50.420
of keys that provides you a
subset of functionality that

00:31:50.420 --> 00:31:52.340
act a little bit as
the user, but not

00:31:52.340 --> 00:31:54.770
in all particular accounts.

00:31:54.770 --> 00:31:58.580
So when a Hadoop cluster runs
a jump under my credentials

00:31:58.580 --> 00:32:02.940
and Lohit is an administrator,
he can see the task running,

00:32:02.940 --> 00:32:06.050
but he cannot access my
Google account on Gmail

00:32:06.050 --> 00:32:07.005
or in my Google Drive.

00:32:07.005 --> 00:32:09.380
SAMEER FAROOQUI: But these
are just normal Google service

00:32:09.380 --> 00:32:09.740
accounts.

00:32:09.740 --> 00:32:11.365
It sounds like you're
multiplexing them

00:32:11.365 --> 00:32:13.640
within the same
cluster to some extent.

00:32:13.640 --> 00:32:16.338
You're sharing many of them
within the same cluster.

00:32:16.338 --> 00:32:18.880
VRUSHALI CHANNAPATTAN: So there
is one Google service account

00:32:18.880 --> 00:32:19.650
per Unix user.

00:32:19.650 --> 00:32:20.900
SAMEER FAROOQUI: Per user, OK.

00:32:20.900 --> 00:32:23.210
VRUSHALI CHANNAPATTAN:
And only that Unix user

00:32:23.210 --> 00:32:27.207
can read the keys or access the
keys of that service account.

00:32:27.207 --> 00:32:28.790
SAMEER FAROOQUI:
Yeah, but, Joep, that

00:32:28.790 --> 00:32:30.110
sounds like a security flaw.

00:32:30.110 --> 00:32:32.990
I mean, if you have one
service account per user,

00:32:32.990 --> 00:32:35.515
what if that gets compromised?

00:32:35.515 --> 00:32:37.890
JOEP ROTTINGHUIS: Well keys
getting compromised is always

00:32:37.890 --> 00:32:38.890
a bad situation.

00:32:38.890 --> 00:32:40.470
In this case, it's
actually better

00:32:40.470 --> 00:32:42.090
if a key gets
compromised to have

00:32:42.090 --> 00:32:43.230
the shadow key compromised.

00:32:43.230 --> 00:32:46.110
Because then the user can
access only GCS and not

00:32:46.110 --> 00:32:47.027
their G Suite account.

00:32:47.027 --> 00:32:49.152
In addition to that, we
end up rotating these keys.

00:32:49.152 --> 00:32:50.860
So it becomes a
little bit easier.

00:32:50.860 --> 00:32:53.040
This is transparent for
each individual user.

00:32:53.040 --> 00:32:55.430
So when a user logs
in or SSHs into a box

00:32:55.430 --> 00:32:58.350
and they access a Hadoop
cluster or they access GCS,

00:32:58.350 --> 00:33:01.270
transparently behind the
scenes the shadow account keys

00:33:01.270 --> 00:33:02.440
are being leveraged.

00:33:02.440 --> 00:33:04.290
And those keys can
rotate over time.

00:33:04.290 --> 00:33:06.270
And they're valid
only for a few days.

00:33:06.270 --> 00:33:08.460
That is much simpler than
their G Suite account keys

00:33:08.460 --> 00:33:10.650
or other keys that may
have to live longer

00:33:10.650 --> 00:33:12.123
and that users
could be aware of.

00:33:12.123 --> 00:33:14.040
SAMEER FAROOQUI: So just
stepping back, Lohit,

00:33:14.040 --> 00:33:16.860
can you walk me through
the user experience?

00:33:16.860 --> 00:33:19.170
Imagine I'm an
engineer at Twitter.

00:33:19.170 --> 00:33:20.940
I want to submit a Hadoop job.

00:33:20.940 --> 00:33:23.130
And now I want to submit
it to Google cloud.

00:33:23.130 --> 00:33:24.870
How is my user
experience different?

00:33:24.870 --> 00:33:25.560
What am I doing?

00:33:25.560 --> 00:33:27.210
Am I SSH-ing into something new?

00:33:27.210 --> 00:33:29.940
Am I submitting it
through a browser?

00:33:29.940 --> 00:33:31.830
How do I actually
submit a Hadoop job

00:33:31.830 --> 00:33:33.898
once Twitter is in Google Cloud?

00:33:33.898 --> 00:33:35.940
LOHIT VIJAYARENU: So that's
a very good question.

00:33:35.940 --> 00:33:39.830
Most of the foundational stuff
which we have been building

00:33:39.830 --> 00:33:44.640
is to make sure that users
have a uniform way of accessing

00:33:44.640 --> 00:33:47.340
the stuff in GCS just like
how they do internally.

00:33:47.340 --> 00:33:49.770
So what we ended up doing
in this particular case

00:33:49.770 --> 00:33:53.700
is that we created
something called jump hosts,

00:33:53.700 --> 00:33:56.077
or call like nest
boxes where users log

00:33:56.077 --> 00:33:59.400
in using their Unix account.

00:33:59.400 --> 00:34:03.390
Once they log into that
box, automatically we

00:34:03.390 --> 00:34:08.670
provision their shadow account's
JSON key only for them.

00:34:08.670 --> 00:34:11.610
It will be accessible
only for them,

00:34:11.610 --> 00:34:14.880
using which they can access
any of the GCP resources.

00:34:14.880 --> 00:34:17.620
Along with that,
some of the resources

00:34:17.620 --> 00:34:21.480
we also provision their
G Suite permissions

00:34:21.480 --> 00:34:22.810
to access some data.

00:34:22.810 --> 00:34:24.960
So if there are tools such
as BigQuery where they

00:34:24.960 --> 00:34:27.389
have to access it from UI--

00:34:27.389 --> 00:34:32.273
so since the access to the data
and the tables, in this case,

00:34:32.273 --> 00:34:33.690
have been granted
permission, they

00:34:33.690 --> 00:34:37.469
will be able to access either
via web browser or these jump

00:34:37.469 --> 00:34:38.070
hosts.

00:34:38.070 --> 00:34:39.570
SAMEER FAROOQUI:
One last question--

00:34:39.570 --> 00:34:42.030
I mean, before we
go to Asheem, who

00:34:42.030 --> 00:34:44.280
seems to-- there's another
question from the audience.

00:34:44.280 --> 00:34:47.010
But before that, you
mentioned the nest box.

00:34:47.010 --> 00:34:48.620
You SSH into the nest box.

00:34:48.620 --> 00:34:51.085
You submit the job.

00:34:51.085 --> 00:34:52.960
On premise, I'm assuming
it's the same thing.

00:34:52.960 --> 00:34:53.940
So you're saying the
user's experience

00:34:53.940 --> 00:34:55.320
is kind of very similar.

00:34:55.320 --> 00:34:56.403
LOHIT VIJAYARENU: Exactly.

00:34:56.403 --> 00:35:00.240
So the same tools which
users have used on prem,

00:35:00.240 --> 00:35:03.720
they are made available on
those nest boxes, as well.

00:35:03.720 --> 00:35:06.860
SAMEER FAROOQUI:
But what about GCS?

00:35:06.860 --> 00:35:10.260
Can users still access GCS
through the Google Cloud

00:35:10.260 --> 00:35:11.807
console through
their web browser?

00:35:11.807 --> 00:35:13.140
LOHIT VIJAYARENU: Yes, they can.

00:35:13.140 --> 00:35:15.790
And they can also use the--
as I have mentioned earlier,

00:35:15.790 --> 00:35:18.790
there is this Google
Hadoop connector.

00:35:18.790 --> 00:35:22.140
So using that, we have created
mappings for the users.

00:35:22.140 --> 00:35:26.820
So just like they access HDFS
using Hadoop commands and then

00:35:26.820 --> 00:35:30.060
a well defined path, we have
created a new [INAUDIBLE] point

00:35:30.060 --> 00:35:34.283
for the GCS to say /GCS and then
any path within [INAUDIBLE]..

00:35:34.283 --> 00:35:36.700
SAMEER FAROOQUI: I see, so
instead of HDFS name node name,

00:35:36.700 --> 00:35:38.203
it's just GS, I believe.

00:35:38.203 --> 00:35:39.120
LOHIT VIJAYARENU: Yes.

00:35:39.120 --> 00:35:40.320
SAMEER FAROOQUI: So it's
kind of transparent.

00:35:40.320 --> 00:35:41.700
LOHIT VIJAYARENU: Exactly.

00:35:41.700 --> 00:35:43.740
SAMEER FAROOQUI: Asheem,
do you have a question?

00:35:43.740 --> 00:35:45.990
ASHEEM: There are two last
questions before we open it

00:35:45.990 --> 00:35:47.610
up to the floor later.

00:35:47.610 --> 00:35:50.053
First, since
BigQuery came up, did

00:35:50.053 --> 00:35:52.470
you think of replacing parts
or all of your Hadoop cluster

00:35:52.470 --> 00:35:55.180
with BigQuery and Bigtable?

00:35:55.180 --> 00:35:56.930
JOEP ROTTINGHUIS: The
short answer is yes.

00:35:56.930 --> 00:35:59.910
We are thinking we will probably
have a combination of both.

00:35:59.910 --> 00:36:02.410
So some use cases they will be
more appropriate on BigQuery.

00:36:02.410 --> 00:36:04.600
Other cases, Presto is
more appropriate for us.

00:36:04.600 --> 00:36:08.500
And other cases, users are still
using Hadoop to run their jobs.

00:36:08.500 --> 00:36:10.000
Because they're doing analysis.

00:36:10.000 --> 00:36:12.962
And then later on, they will
run that same job in production.

00:36:12.962 --> 00:36:14.920
SAMEER FAROOQUI: Joep,
how do your users decide

00:36:14.920 --> 00:36:18.370
if they want to run a SQL
query through Presto, or Spark,

00:36:18.370 --> 00:36:19.932
or Hive?

00:36:19.932 --> 00:36:21.390
JOEP ROTTINGHUIS:
The exact details

00:36:21.390 --> 00:36:22.630
of how we're going
to decide between all

00:36:22.630 --> 00:36:24.570
of the various cases, we're
going to still determine.

00:36:24.570 --> 00:36:25.403
And we're doing an--

00:36:25.403 --> 00:36:27.420
we're going to run an
earlier alpha test mode

00:36:27.420 --> 00:36:28.850
now with some of our users.

00:36:28.850 --> 00:36:31.753
And we're going to find out what
the most appropriate tool is.

00:36:31.753 --> 00:36:32.670
SAMEER FAROOQUI: Cool.

00:36:32.670 --> 00:36:34.128
Was there another
question, Asheem?

00:36:34.128 --> 00:36:38.430
ASHEEM: And last question from
online is this must cost a ton.

00:36:38.430 --> 00:36:41.250
Do you have any tips
to take advantage

00:36:41.250 --> 00:36:43.930
of how to keep the
costs under control,

00:36:43.930 --> 00:36:46.370
particularly with regards
to Dataproc and GCS?

00:36:50.150 --> 00:36:52.760
VRUSHALI CHANNAPATTAN: So I
think being in the cloud model,

00:36:52.760 --> 00:36:57.527
being in GCP, there are a
lot of advantages to that.

00:36:57.527 --> 00:36:59.360
Rather than looking at
just the cost, if you

00:36:59.360 --> 00:37:02.780
look at the advantages that
we get by Google's networking

00:37:02.780 --> 00:37:03.500
setup--

00:37:03.500 --> 00:37:06.890
the fact that we can separate
compute and storage, right--

00:37:06.890 --> 00:37:08.630
all of those advantages add up.

00:37:08.630 --> 00:37:11.870
So it's really difficult to
say whether we are focusing

00:37:11.870 --> 00:37:15.050
on the storage cost versus
all of these other advantages

00:37:15.050 --> 00:37:16.158
that we are getting.

00:37:16.158 --> 00:37:18.200
JOEP ROTTINGHUIS: This is
also one of the reasons

00:37:18.200 --> 00:37:20.540
why we're running a larger
shared Hadoop cluster where

00:37:20.540 --> 00:37:22.995
individual jobs can
interleave together.

00:37:22.995 --> 00:37:23.870
So if there's a job--

00:37:23.870 --> 00:37:26.090
there's multiple stages
of a MapReduce job,

00:37:26.090 --> 00:37:29.170
and the job's, for example, at
a stage with a single reducer,

00:37:29.170 --> 00:37:31.070
the cluster's not going
to sit around idle.

00:37:31.070 --> 00:37:33.880
Because other users will
use that same cluster.

00:37:33.880 --> 00:37:36.260
We'll end up being able
to very closely monitor

00:37:36.260 --> 00:37:38.753
which users use which
resources and have a mapping.

00:37:38.753 --> 00:37:40.170
And at the end our
users are going

00:37:40.170 --> 00:37:41.480
to be responsible--
our engineers are

00:37:41.480 --> 00:37:42.980
responsible to
make sure that they

00:37:42.980 --> 00:37:44.680
use the tools in an
appropriate manner.

00:37:44.680 --> 00:37:46.430
This is the same as
what we do on premise.

00:37:46.430 --> 00:37:48.650
Sometimes users will run jobs--

00:37:48.650 --> 00:37:50.960
we sometimes see jobs
that are running 300,000

00:37:50.960 --> 00:37:53.210
mappers and 200,000 reducers.

00:37:53.210 --> 00:37:54.170
This can happen.

00:37:54.170 --> 00:37:55.932
And it's a balance
between making

00:37:55.932 --> 00:37:57.890
it as simple as we can
for users to use and not

00:37:57.890 --> 00:38:00.382
have to think about it
and, at the same time,

00:38:00.382 --> 00:38:02.090
if the cost becomes
very high, then users

00:38:02.090 --> 00:38:04.190
have to rationalize
and think through,

00:38:04.190 --> 00:38:06.830
is this the most appropriate
thing to do for a business?

00:38:06.830 --> 00:38:07.940
And they make that choice.

00:38:07.940 --> 00:38:08.990
SAMEER FAROOQUI: You
mentioned something

00:38:08.990 --> 00:38:11.502
that I think is worth diving
a little bit more into.

00:38:11.502 --> 00:38:12.960
You mentioned
separation of compute

00:38:12.960 --> 00:38:15.230
from storage, which
is not what you

00:38:15.230 --> 00:38:18.080
learn about when you read like
Hadoop, the Definitive Guide.

00:38:18.080 --> 00:38:21.920
The whole idea of Hadoop is to
keep storage close to compute.

00:38:21.920 --> 00:38:23.930
There's many benefits
there, like the map task

00:38:23.930 --> 00:38:26.480
can start near where one of
the three replicas of the data

00:38:26.480 --> 00:38:27.680
block is.

00:38:27.680 --> 00:38:31.520
When you separated out
Hadoop at your scale,

00:38:31.520 --> 00:38:37.580
separate out storage onto
GCS and then separately ran

00:38:37.580 --> 00:38:39.470
Hadoop clusters--

00:38:39.470 --> 00:38:42.260
I think-- can you share
more about your thinking

00:38:42.260 --> 00:38:45.290
there and around any challenges
you ran into when you

00:38:45.290 --> 00:38:46.830
did something at that scale?

00:38:46.830 --> 00:38:50.840
And I'm also curious, when
you store something in GCS,

00:38:50.840 --> 00:38:53.120
can one Hadoop cluster
read from that?

00:38:53.120 --> 00:38:55.280
Can many Hadoop
clusters read from that?

00:38:55.280 --> 00:38:57.518
And just talk about the
pros and cons about that.

00:38:57.518 --> 00:38:59.810
JOEP ROTTINGHUIS: Well, I'll
answer the second question

00:38:59.810 --> 00:39:01.393
first, because that's
the simpler one.

00:39:01.393 --> 00:39:04.100
And the first question
I'll hand over to Lohit.

00:39:04.100 --> 00:39:07.130
Yes, one of the reasons
why we stored data in GCS

00:39:07.130 --> 00:39:09.670
is because then it can be
shared across multiple users

00:39:09.670 --> 00:39:11.170
if they have the
appropriate access.

00:39:11.170 --> 00:39:14.090
So there's access
models around that.

00:39:14.090 --> 00:39:16.670
So data stored in GCS can
be used by multiple users

00:39:16.670 --> 00:39:17.700
from various clusters.

00:39:17.700 --> 00:39:19.420
That's the one
mechanism how to share.

00:39:19.420 --> 00:39:21.003
SAMEER FAROOQUI:
Across many projects.

00:39:21.003 --> 00:39:24.510
So one GCS bucket can serve
two external projects, as well?

00:39:24.510 --> 00:39:25.847
JOEP ROTTINGHUIS: That's right.

00:39:25.847 --> 00:39:27.430
SAMEER FAROOQUI: So
that goes back to,

00:39:27.430 --> 00:39:31.250
I think, this slide, where you
have one GCS in like Twitter

00:39:31.250 --> 00:39:31.750
product.

00:39:31.750 --> 00:39:33.610
But the revenue team
can read from that

00:39:33.610 --> 00:39:34.777
if permissions were scoped.

00:39:34.777 --> 00:39:35.860
JOEP ROTTINGHUIS: Correct.

00:39:35.860 --> 00:39:36.940
SAMEER FAROOQUI: I see.

00:39:36.940 --> 00:39:40.060
So Lohit, can you talk
more about that decision

00:39:40.060 --> 00:39:43.780
to separate compute from storage
around any technical challenges

00:39:43.780 --> 00:39:45.690
you saw there?

00:39:45.690 --> 00:39:47.250
LOHIT VIJAYARENU:
So traditionally,

00:39:47.250 --> 00:39:53.750
like HDFS when it became very
popular over the last decade--

00:39:53.750 --> 00:39:58.330
so the local
compute was the key.

00:39:58.330 --> 00:40:00.790
Basically, wherever
the data is, you

00:40:00.790 --> 00:40:04.120
send your compute there
to be more efficient.

00:40:04.120 --> 00:40:06.310
And over the years,
what happened

00:40:06.310 --> 00:40:08.450
was you build these
large clusters,

00:40:08.450 --> 00:40:10.850
say 2,000, 5,000 node clusters.

00:40:10.850 --> 00:40:12.940
And if you need
more compute, there

00:40:12.940 --> 00:40:16.690
was no other option other than
either changing your machine

00:40:16.690 --> 00:40:21.390
configuration or adding extra
nodes with more disk anyways,

00:40:21.390 --> 00:40:21.890
right?

00:40:21.890 --> 00:40:22.807
SAMEER FAROOQUI: Sure.

00:40:22.807 --> 00:40:24.550
LOHIT VIJAYARENU:
So that model kind

00:40:24.550 --> 00:40:27.760
of changed altogether when
the storage and the compute

00:40:27.760 --> 00:40:31.240
could be now separated
independently.

00:40:31.240 --> 00:40:33.610
So for example, one
example is that we

00:40:33.610 --> 00:40:35.590
tend to replicate
the same data set

00:40:35.590 --> 00:40:38.680
across multiple different
clusters based on use cases.

00:40:38.680 --> 00:40:42.010
But with the storage
system such as GCS,

00:40:42.010 --> 00:40:43.510
you just store one copy.

00:40:43.510 --> 00:40:46.810
And then multiple
compute instances

00:40:46.810 --> 00:40:49.220
can independently access them.

00:40:49.220 --> 00:40:52.940
So there is definitely a bit
of a performance difference.

00:40:52.940 --> 00:40:56.530
But, for the most part,
when you factor in

00:40:56.530 --> 00:41:00.310
how the network is laid out in
GCP, and how you're caching,

00:41:00.310 --> 00:41:06.072
and how you're organizing
your jobs, it kind of adds up.

00:41:06.072 --> 00:41:07.030
SAMEER FAROOQUI: I see.

00:41:07.030 --> 00:41:09.400
You saw no performance
degradations in doing

00:41:09.400 --> 00:41:10.587
that type of separation?

00:41:10.587 --> 00:41:12.670
LOHIT VIJAYARENU: For most
of the batch analytics,

00:41:12.670 --> 00:41:16.750
when we did the performance
evaluation last year--

00:41:16.750 --> 00:41:20.050
Derek and Dave also shared
a lot of information

00:41:20.050 --> 00:41:21.820
about that in
their Google NEXT--

00:41:21.820 --> 00:41:26.860
for the most part, we saw
very consistent performance

00:41:26.860 --> 00:41:28.060
from GCP.

00:41:28.060 --> 00:41:30.435
And then it was
almost one is to one.

00:41:30.435 --> 00:41:31.310
SAMEER FAROOQUI: Wow.

00:41:31.310 --> 00:41:33.880
I definitely want to
plug Derek Lyons' talk

00:41:33.880 --> 00:41:35.620
on YouTube about this, as well.

00:41:35.620 --> 00:41:37.450
You guys did a very
sophisticated analysis

00:41:37.450 --> 00:41:40.810
with like GridMix and
Terra-Gen to understand

00:41:40.810 --> 00:41:45.130
your synthetic simulation use
cases within Google Cloud.

00:41:45.130 --> 00:41:47.443
So definitely do a
YouTube search on that.

00:41:47.443 --> 00:41:49.360
So before we take questions
from the audience,

00:41:49.360 --> 00:41:53.050
I want to talk a bit about
the new tools or Google Cloud

00:41:53.050 --> 00:41:55.720
Platform improvements
that were needed

00:41:55.720 --> 00:41:57.936
to support this migration.

00:41:57.936 --> 00:42:00.732
Lohit, can you talk about the
data retention, for example?

00:42:00.732 --> 00:42:01.690
LOHIT VIJAYARENU: Sure.

00:42:01.690 --> 00:42:08.290
So GCS already provided Object
Lifecycle Management tools

00:42:08.290 --> 00:42:11.830
to manage your data to
automatically delete it

00:42:11.830 --> 00:42:15.100
based on, say, create
time, or something.

00:42:15.100 --> 00:42:17.410
But for us, it is
much more complicated.

00:42:17.410 --> 00:42:19.670
As I mentioned earlier,
we have data sets

00:42:19.670 --> 00:42:22.510
which have an hourly partition.

00:42:22.510 --> 00:42:25.750
And users usually configure
complext retential tools

00:42:25.750 --> 00:42:29.590
such as any data which is
older than, say, 30 days, needs

00:42:29.590 --> 00:42:31.430
to be deleted automatically.

00:42:31.430 --> 00:42:35.890
And that decision
happens based on the path

00:42:35.890 --> 00:42:39.370
of that data set, not the actual
create time or modification

00:42:39.370 --> 00:42:40.240
time.

00:42:40.240 --> 00:42:44.660
It's derived out of the path
of the particular data set.

00:42:44.660 --> 00:42:47.560
Because there are cases
where we could backfill.

00:42:47.560 --> 00:42:50.700
We could copy the entire data
set to a destination cluster.

00:42:50.700 --> 00:42:53.930
Or we could scrub the
data to make some changes.

00:42:53.930 --> 00:42:58.120
So such a functionality
was not available on GCS.

00:42:58.120 --> 00:43:01.540
So with the Google
engineers, there

00:43:01.540 --> 00:43:07.720
is now an open source tool
called SDRS, supplementary data

00:43:07.720 --> 00:43:10.930
retention service, which is also
open source, which kind of does

00:43:10.930 --> 00:43:13.447
a similar thing on GCP, as well.

00:43:13.447 --> 00:43:15.280
SAMEER FAROOQUI: Where
is it open source at?

00:43:15.280 --> 00:43:17.080
LOHIT VIJAYARENU: Its on GitHub.

00:43:17.080 --> 00:43:19.600
SAMEER FAROOQUI: I believe
it's in Google Cloud Platform

00:43:19.600 --> 00:43:20.370
GitHub.

00:43:20.370 --> 00:43:23.290
OK, so I'm trying to understand
a little bit more around this.

00:43:23.290 --> 00:43:26.620
You're saying Object Lifecycle
Management in GCS indeed

00:43:26.620 --> 00:43:29.170
works on object creation time.

00:43:29.170 --> 00:43:33.252
You showed us hourly time
partitioned data sets before.

00:43:33.252 --> 00:43:34.960
You're saying those
are like event times?

00:43:34.960 --> 00:43:36.160
Event of what?

00:43:36.160 --> 00:43:37.628
Event of when the
tweet took place?

00:43:37.628 --> 00:43:39.670
LOHIT VIJAYARENU: So those
are the times in which

00:43:39.670 --> 00:43:41.998
that message got created.

00:43:41.998 --> 00:43:43.790
SAMEER FAROOQUI: On
the real time clusters?

00:43:43.790 --> 00:43:44.630
LOHIT VIJAYARENU: On
the real time clusters.

00:43:44.630 --> 00:43:46.297
SAMEER FAROOQUI: So
that's time stamped.

00:43:46.297 --> 00:43:49.130
And you want to expire
data based on that time,

00:43:49.130 --> 00:43:51.170
not the object
creation time, in case

00:43:51.170 --> 00:43:53.347
there's delays on the log
pipeline, I'm guessing.

00:43:53.347 --> 00:43:54.680
LOHIT VIJAYARENU: That is right.

00:43:54.680 --> 00:44:00.030
So as I said, when we copy the
data from our HDFS cluster--

00:44:00.030 --> 00:44:03.710
lets take an example
of our initial copy.

00:44:03.710 --> 00:44:07.370
The seed copy we would copy is
a few months of data onto GCS.

00:44:07.370 --> 00:44:11.810
There we cannot set OLM policy
saying that anything older than

00:44:11.810 --> 00:44:13.370
30 days, get rid of it.

00:44:13.370 --> 00:44:14.180
SAMEER FAROOQUI:
It's just all new.

00:44:14.180 --> 00:44:15.263
LOHIT VIJAYARENU: Exactly.

00:44:15.263 --> 00:44:18.260
So that is the reason why we
had to come up with this tool.

00:44:18.260 --> 00:44:20.330
SAMEER FAROOQUI: Interesting.

00:44:20.330 --> 00:44:22.040
What about demigods, Vrushali?

00:44:22.040 --> 00:44:24.360
That's an interesting
sounding name.

00:44:24.360 --> 00:44:26.845
Many gods-- what is that?

00:44:26.845 --> 00:44:28.220
VRUSHALI CHANNAPATTAN:
So demigod

00:44:28.220 --> 00:44:30.500
is basically a group
of services that we

00:44:30.500 --> 00:44:35.560
use to configure Twitter's GCP
platform for partly cloudy.

00:44:35.560 --> 00:44:37.370
The name demigod
comes from the fact

00:44:37.370 --> 00:44:40.340
that these are admin services.

00:44:40.340 --> 00:44:42.150
These are admin processes.

00:44:42.150 --> 00:44:45.230
But they have privileges
that are exactly scoped

00:44:45.230 --> 00:44:46.920
to whatever they need to do.

00:44:46.920 --> 00:44:47.960
So it's like a demigod.

00:44:47.960 --> 00:44:52.050
It's a godlike service, but
not quite all of the admin

00:44:52.050 --> 00:44:54.562
privileges, but only that
which-- what it needs to do.

00:44:54.562 --> 00:44:56.270
SAMEER FAROOQUI: What
do they do, though?

00:44:56.270 --> 00:44:57.950
They have semi admin privileges.

00:44:57.950 --> 00:44:58.910
What do they do?

00:44:58.910 --> 00:45:00.650
VRUSHALI CHANNAPATTAN:
So these services

00:45:00.650 --> 00:45:03.020
are responsible for creating
buckets, creating shadow

00:45:03.020 --> 00:45:07.340
accounts, granting IAM policies
on buckets, key management,

00:45:07.340 --> 00:45:08.290
all of that.

00:45:08.290 --> 00:45:10.358
SAMEER FAROOQUI: Oh,
interesting, so provisioning

00:45:10.358 --> 00:45:11.900
those service accounts
that we talked

00:45:11.900 --> 00:45:16.840
about earlier, or making these
hundreds or thousands of GCS

00:45:16.840 --> 00:45:17.340
buckets.

00:45:17.340 --> 00:45:19.520
It sounds like automation tools?

00:45:19.520 --> 00:45:20.450
Is that fair to say?

00:45:20.450 --> 00:45:21.575
VRUSHALI CHANNAPATTAN: Yes.

00:45:21.575 --> 00:45:24.290
So these are automated
tools that Google--

00:45:24.290 --> 00:45:27.290
that we have that
are running in GCP.

00:45:27.290 --> 00:45:29.960
These are event driven services.

00:45:29.960 --> 00:45:32.120
So, for example, when
users join LDAP groups

00:45:32.120 --> 00:45:34.580
or when we make config
changes to our files,

00:45:34.580 --> 00:45:37.850
that's when these services
get triggered, and they run.

00:45:37.850 --> 00:45:40.070
And then they reset
the state of the system

00:45:40.070 --> 00:45:42.020
to what it's expected to be.

00:45:42.020 --> 00:45:45.110
So if somebody makes a
manual change on a bucket,

00:45:45.110 --> 00:45:47.150
the demigod service
will go and reset

00:45:47.150 --> 00:45:49.485
the state to whatever
it should be.

00:45:49.485 --> 00:45:51.610
SAMEER FAROOQUI: Interesting,
what about the bucket

00:45:51.610 --> 00:45:53.493
move rename tool?

00:45:53.493 --> 00:45:55.910
VRUSHALI CHANNAPATTAN: So, as
we were working with buckets

00:45:55.910 --> 00:45:58.310
and getting familiar
with GCP, we

00:45:58.310 --> 00:46:00.500
realized that we
needed the ability

00:46:00.500 --> 00:46:03.530
to move buckets across
projects or to change

00:46:03.530 --> 00:46:04.760
the name of buckets.

00:46:04.760 --> 00:46:06.410
We wanted to work
with buckets as we

00:46:06.410 --> 00:46:08.420
were working on our design.

00:46:08.420 --> 00:46:11.270
That is when we wanted
a tool from Google.

00:46:11.270 --> 00:46:13.490
And we put forward
this request to Google.

00:46:13.490 --> 00:46:15.560
And they built an
open source tool.

00:46:15.560 --> 00:46:17.710
It's on GitHub, as well.

00:46:17.710 --> 00:46:19.910
So it can move buckets
across projects.

00:46:19.910 --> 00:46:21.420
It can rename them.

00:46:21.420 --> 00:46:24.680
There are several configuration
options that it takes.

00:46:24.680 --> 00:46:26.260
Yeah, feel free to look it up.

00:46:26.260 --> 00:46:27.230
SAMEER FAROOQUI: And
that's on GitHub,

00:46:27.230 --> 00:46:29.310
I believe, for Google
Cloud Platform, as well.

00:46:29.310 --> 00:46:30.920
I think that's a
really useful tool.

00:46:30.920 --> 00:46:33.530
GCS buckets are
globally namespaced.

00:46:33.530 --> 00:46:35.673
So I think you have
to have just one name.

00:46:35.673 --> 00:46:37.340
So I'm guessing this
tool does something

00:46:37.340 --> 00:46:41.633
like deletes that name and
stores the data temporarily,

00:46:41.633 --> 00:46:43.550
and recreates that name
in a different project

00:46:43.550 --> 00:46:44.900
and just moves it over?

00:46:44.900 --> 00:46:46.025
VRUSHALI CHANNAPATTAN: Yes.

00:46:46.025 --> 00:46:49.280
So this tool basically creates
a temporary swap bucket.

00:46:49.280 --> 00:46:51.630
It first copies over
all of the data.

00:46:51.630 --> 00:46:53.150
And when it starts
that, it actually

00:46:53.150 --> 00:46:55.070
locks the incoming
bucket so that you don't

00:46:55.070 --> 00:46:56.900
keep making changes there.

00:46:56.900 --> 00:46:59.690
And then, once all of the
data has been copied over,

00:46:59.690 --> 00:47:02.780
it will delete this bucket and
then recreate the same bucket

00:47:02.780 --> 00:47:05.540
in a different project
or with a different name,

00:47:05.540 --> 00:47:08.875
and then copy over the data from
the temporary bucket into that.

00:47:08.875 --> 00:47:10.625
SAMEER FAROOQUI: That
sounds super useful.

00:47:10.625 --> 00:47:12.890
Joep, can you tell us a
little bit about the HDFS

00:47:12.890 --> 00:47:14.608
to GCS checksum feature?

00:47:14.608 --> 00:47:16.400
JOEP ROTTINGHUIS: Yeah,
this is one feature

00:47:16.400 --> 00:47:18.950
that we added in
collaboration with Google

00:47:18.950 --> 00:47:20.930
in order to make sure
that the checksums match

00:47:20.930 --> 00:47:23.513
all the way from the source all
the way through to the target.

00:47:23.513 --> 00:47:26.940
The checksumming mechanism in
HDFS is different than in GCS.

00:47:26.940 --> 00:47:29.675
And this is a feature that's
been added HDFS side to make

00:47:29.675 --> 00:47:31.550
sure that the checksums
can check all the way

00:47:31.550 --> 00:47:32.780
through from end to end.

00:47:32.780 --> 00:47:33.590
SAMEER FAROOQUI: Very cool.

00:47:33.590 --> 00:47:35.923
And I believe this work was
actually done in open source

00:47:35.923 --> 00:47:37.298
Apache HDFS, correct?

00:47:37.298 --> 00:47:39.590
JOEP ROTTINGHUIS: Yes,
initially one of my team members

00:47:39.590 --> 00:47:41.542
implemented this first
and is running that.

00:47:41.542 --> 00:47:43.250
Because not all of
our clusters have been

00:47:43.250 --> 00:47:44.458
upgraded to that version yet.

00:47:44.458 --> 00:47:46.042
And they worked with
Google engineers.

00:47:46.042 --> 00:47:47.720
And that's done in
open source, yes.

00:47:47.720 --> 00:47:48.290
SAMEER FAROOQUI: Very cool.

00:47:48.290 --> 00:47:49.707
And finally, Joep,
can you tell us

00:47:49.707 --> 00:47:52.425
a little bit about GCS connector
2.0 performance improvements?

00:47:52.425 --> 00:47:54.050
I should tell-- let
the audience know--

00:47:54.050 --> 00:47:56.030
that in the next
week or two, we are

00:47:56.030 --> 00:47:58.580
going to increment the
version of GCS connector

00:47:58.580 --> 00:48:00.710
from 1.x to 2.0.

00:48:00.710 --> 00:48:04.930
And there's a couple of dozen
major improvements in there,

00:48:04.930 --> 00:48:08.090
a lot of which were driven
by the Twitter project.

00:48:08.090 --> 00:48:10.970
Can you tell us a little bit
about some of that, Joep?

00:48:10.970 --> 00:48:12.290
JOEP ROTTINGHUIS: Yeah, one of
the features that's been added

00:48:12.290 --> 00:48:14.480
is this cooperative
locking mechanism feature

00:48:14.480 --> 00:48:16.872
where you can imagine an
object store functions very

00:48:16.872 --> 00:48:18.080
different than a file system.

00:48:18.080 --> 00:48:20.520
In an object store, there is
no such thing as an i note

00:48:20.520 --> 00:48:22.950
where directories are
a first class citizen.

00:48:22.950 --> 00:48:25.460
So, as items move
around, it's possible

00:48:25.460 --> 00:48:27.773
that people move directories.

00:48:27.773 --> 00:48:30.190
SAMEER FAROOQUI: Cool, so it
locks a directory, I suppose,

00:48:30.190 --> 00:48:34.847
before it modifies
that directory?

00:48:34.847 --> 00:48:36.430
JOEP ROTTINGHUIS:
Yup, a lock is taken

00:48:36.430 --> 00:48:39.160
so that multiple uses
moving around directories

00:48:39.160 --> 00:48:39.580
don't collide with one another.

00:48:39.580 --> 00:48:40.450
SAMEER FAROOQUI:
And I believe this

00:48:40.450 --> 00:48:42.010
is in alpha stage right now.

00:48:42.010 --> 00:48:43.990
But it should be
coming out very soon.

00:48:43.990 --> 00:48:46.930
All right, so, to start
wrapping up, Joep,

00:48:46.930 --> 00:48:49.180
can you tell us a little
bit about this special URL

00:48:49.180 --> 00:48:52.130
that you had talked about
at the very beginning?

00:48:52.130 --> 00:48:54.130
JOEP ROTTINGHUIS: Yeah,
Google is hosting a page

00:48:54.130 --> 00:48:58.380
on cloud.google.com/twitter.

00:48:58.380 --> 00:49:00.765
We'll be hosting a
list of links there,

00:49:00.765 --> 00:49:02.890
and resources, and the
things that we're working on

00:49:02.890 --> 00:49:04.750
together and collaborating on.

00:49:04.750 --> 00:49:06.440
We're working on a
series of blog posts.

00:49:06.440 --> 00:49:09.185
So those we'll be
linking there as well.

00:49:09.185 --> 00:49:10.810
And all the talks
will be linked there.

00:49:10.810 --> 00:49:13.227
So, if people want to follow
along and collaborate with us

00:49:13.227 --> 00:49:15.520
on these projects, they can
find all this information

00:49:15.520 --> 00:49:16.020
over there.

00:49:16.020 --> 00:49:17.437
SAMEER FAROOQUI:
Cool, and yeah, I

00:49:17.437 --> 00:49:20.110
would love to see a lot
more of the details of what

00:49:20.110 --> 00:49:22.630
you guys are doing in
the next year or so.

00:49:22.630 --> 00:49:25.900
I did want to give a plug to
a few upcoming Twitter talks

00:49:25.900 --> 00:49:29.583
in the next couple of days.

00:49:29.583 --> 00:49:31.000
So take a quick
screenshot of this

00:49:31.000 --> 00:49:33.610
if you guys are interested.

00:49:33.610 --> 00:49:38.020
And, for the live Q&amp;A,
check out Twitter.

